# Cloudflare Pages serves an existing static asset at a matching path BEFORE
# it evaluates _redirects rules — a rule here can never suppress a file that
# is actually deployed at that exact path. That's why the /.htaccess rule
# below used to be a no-op: .htaccess was a real deployed file, so Cloudflare
# served it directly and never reached this rule. It has since been deleted
# from the repo (it had no effect on Cloudflare Pages anyway, and duplicated
# _headers) rather than kept as a file this platform can't actually block;
# it's still recoverable from git history if ever needed for an Apache host.
#
# The /templates/* rule has the same limitation and is still a no-op today:
# templates/navbar.html and templates/footer.html are genuinely deployed
# (build.js reads them from this path at build time), so Cloudflare serves
# them directly rather than the 404 below. Fixing that for real would mean
# publishing from a separate build output directory that excludes template
# sources — a Cloudflare Pages project-settings change, not a file change,
# so it's left as a documented gap rather than a rule that looks like a fix
# but isn't. robots.txt already disallows /templates/ for crawlers; the two
# files it serves contain no secrets, just nav/footer markup fragments.
/templates/*     /404.html   404
